### <a id="vuln-02"></a>[VULN-02] Path Traversal & Filesystem Probing Oracle in Banner Handler (`b2.php`)

> [!NOTE]
> **Audit Occurrence Disclaimer:** Found in **9 of 9** available audit reports (`8e693d74`, `3643eaa6`, `6bb7274b`, `56468d00`, `88c733dd`, `c6fa5ed1`, `a3b97157`, `5e432690`, `991dcf68`).

| Specification | Metric / Details |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Vulnerability Identifier** | `VULN-02` |
| **Severity Level** | **🟠 High** |
| **Vulnerability Classification** | CWE-22 (Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory), CVSS 7.5 (High) |
| **Primary Target Locations** | `Repository-wide` |

#### Technical Description & Exploit Mechanics

The banner redirect endpoint handles dynamic board banners by reading `$_GET['board']`:
  ```php
  $board = $_GET['board'] ?? 'soy';
  $files = scandir("static/board_banners/$board", SCANDIR_SORT_NONE);
  $files = array_diff($files, ['.', '..']);
  if (empty($files)) {
      header("Location: /static/board_banners/default.png");
      exit;
  }
  $name = $files[array_rand($files)];
  header("Location: /static/board_banners/$board/$name", true, 303);
  ```
  `$board` is concatenated into filesystem paths without directory traversal (`../`) filtering or whitelist validation. An attacker passing `board=../../` can traverse above the web root. If a directory contains files, `scandir()` succeeds and redirects to `/static/board_banners/../../[filename]`, creating an oracle to enumerate server directories and file structures.

