### <a id="vuln-04"></a>[VULN-04] Trivial IP Deanonymization via AES-CTR Keystream Reuse & Static IV

> [!NOTE]
> **Audit Occurrence Disclaimer:** Found in **8 of 9** available audit reports (`8e693d74`, `3643eaa6`, `6bb7274b`, `56468d00`, `88c733dd`, `c6fa5ed1`, `a3b97157`, `991dcf68`).

| Specification | Metric / Details |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Vulnerability Identifier** | `VULN-04` |
| **Severity Level** | **🔴 Critical** |
| **Vulnerability Classification** | CWE-329 (Generation of Predictable IV with CBC/CTR Mode), CWE-327 (Broken Crypto), CVSS 9.1 (Critical) |
| **Primary Target Locations** | [`ip-token.php`](file:///D:/Project/soyjaksource/myshare/ip-token.php)<br>[`pass.php`](file:///D:/Project/soyjaksource/myshare/pass.php)<br>[`inc/functions.php`](file:///D:/Project/soyjaksource/myshare/inc/functions.php) |

#### Technical Description & Exploit Mechanics

User IP cloaking, voice chat participant mapping, and anti-bot token systems use AES-CTR encryption with a hardcoded static initialization vector (IV) or static HMAC salts:
  $$C_1 = P_1 \oplus K_s, \quad C_2 = P_2 \oplus K_s \implies C_1 \oplus C_2 = P_1 \oplus P_2$$
  Because the keystream $K_s$ remains identical across encryption operations, XORing two ciphertexts cancels out the keystream. Given known structure in IPv4 addresses and timestamps, an attacker can trivially recover cleartext visitor IP addresses en masse.

